  "constraintReading": "len(prices) can reach 10⁵, so trying every buy day with every later sell day means about 5·10⁹ pairs: O(n²) is far too slow. Aim for O(n), a single pass over the days. The O(1) space target also rules out extra lists, such as the highest price after each day, so keep only a few numbers as you go.",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Can you sell on the day you buy, or before it? No: the sale comes on a later day. What if the price only falls? Then every trade loses money, so you skip it and return 0. Try [4, 7, 1, 3] by hand: buying at 4 and selling at 7 earns 3, while buying at 1 and selling at 3 earns only 2, so the answer is 3.",
    "approach": "Sliding window, running from the buy day on the left to the sell day on the right. Move the sell day forward one day at a time. For today's sale, the best buy day is the cheapest day before it, so keep `buy` on the lowest price so far. If today's price is lower still, move `buy` to today: every later sale does better buying here. Otherwise, today's profit is today's price minus the price on `buy`; keep the best one. One pass is enough.",
    "whyNot": "The tempting route is to try every buy day with every later sell day. That works, but it's O(n²) time: about 5·10⁹ pairs when there are 10⁵ days. Taking the lowest and the highest price overall doesn't work either: the highest price can come before the lowest one, and you can't sell before you buy.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "`buy` is the window's left end: the day with the lowest price so far. It starts at day 0. `best` starts at 0, because not trading is always allowed.",
      "loop": "Move `sell`, the right end, through the days from day 1 to the last one. Each new day is one more chance to sell.",
      "update": "If today's price is lower than the price on `buy`, move `buy` all the way to today. A lower buy price beats the old one for every later sale, so the window starts over here.",
      "record": "Otherwise, selling today earns today's price minus the price on `buy`. Keep the larger of that profit and `best`.",
      "return": "After the last day, return `best`. It's still 0 if the price never rose after a low."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def maxProfit(self, prices: list[int]) -> int:\n        buy = 0\n        best = 0\n        for sell in range(1, len(prices)):\n            if prices[sell] < prices[buy]:\n                buy = sell  # viz:new_low\n            else:\n                profit = prices[sell] - prices[buy]  # viz:sell\n                best = max(best, profit)\n        return best  # viz:done\n",
    "explanation": "The loop looks at each day once and does a fixed amount of work there, so the time is O(n). You keep only the buy day, today's profit and the best profit, so the extra space is O(1).",
    "toolkit": ["min_max_key"]
  },
